        "en" to GemDetails(
            "The great scallop (Pecten maximus) is a bivalve of the North-East Atlantic and the English Channel that lies on or half-buried in sandy and gravelly bottoms, from a few metres to about a hundred metres deep. It filters plankton and can swim by clapping its valves to escape starfish. The edge of its mantle bears a row of small blue eyes, up to 200, whose mirrors of guanine crystals form images. Its shell, secreted by the mantle, records growth as concentric rings: one annual ring marks the winter growth stop, which reveals its age.",
            "Limy shell of foliated calcite, non-nacreous, 10 to 15 cm, with two different valves: one flat (upper), the other domed (lower), with radiating ribs and two “ears”; hardness about 3; specific gravity 2.7. White, pink, red, brown or mottled. It is not cut: it is used whole as a decorative object, serving dish or craft base; it has been the model for countless architectural and jewellery motifs.",
            "North-East Atlantic: the English Channel (Bay of Saint-Brieuc, Bay of the Seine, Normandy, Brittany), Ireland, Scotland, Norway, the Bay of Biscay down to Portugal. In France fishing is tightly managed: season from October to May, minimum size, licences and limited fishing days, making the Côtes-d'Armor scallop (Label Rouge, PGI) an example of sustainable management. A related Mediterranean species: Pecten jacobaeus.",
            "The emblem of pilgrims to Santiago de Compostela since the Middle Ages, who brought it back from Galicia as proof of their journey and used it as a bowl; it appears on waymarkers of the pilgrim routes, on coats of arms and in Botticelli's “The Birth of Venus”. It is said to accompany journeys and life's transitions. It is associated with the sacral chakra and the sign of Cancer. Clean with lukewarm water and mild soap; avoid acids; cleanse with water or by smudging; recharge in moonlight."
